{
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Writing systems represent language using visual symbols, which may or may not correspond to the sounds of spoken language.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "The Latin alphabet was originally based on the representation of single sounds, so that words were constructed from letters that generally denote a single consonant or vowel.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "In syllabic scripts, each sign represents a whole syllable.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "In logographic scripts, each sign represents an entire word and will generally bear no relation to the sound of that word in spoken language.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Written language represents the way spoken sounds and words follow one after another by arranging symbols according to a pattern that follows a certain direction.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Some writing systems use the horizontal axis, while others use the vertical dimension. A few writing systems use opposite directions for alternating lines.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "In order to represent the sounds of the world's languages in writing, linguists have developed the International Phonetic Alphabet, designed to represent all of the discrete sounds that are known to contribute to meaning in human languages.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0018",
        "title": "Sounds and symbols",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "definition",
      "value": "Grammar is the study of how meaningful elements called morphemes within a language can be combined into utterances.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "morpheme_types",
      "value": "Morphemes can either be free or bound.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "definition_free_morphemes",
      "value": "If they are free to be moved around within an utterance, they are usually called words.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "definition_bound_morphemes",
      "value": "If they are bound to other words or morphemes, they are called affixes.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "rules_definition",
      "value": "The way in which meaningful elements can be combined within a language is governed by rules.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "morphology_definition",
      "value": "The study of the rules for the internal structure of words are called morphology.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "syntax_definition",
      "value": "The rules of the internal structure of phrases and sentences are called syntax.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0019",
        "title": "Grammar",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Grammar can be described as a system of categories and a set of rules that determine how categories combine to form different aspects of meaning. Languages differ widely in whether they are encoded through the use of categories or lexical units. However, several categories are so common as to be nearly universal.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0020",
        "title": "Grammatical categories",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "category": "overview",
      "field": "universal_categories",
      "value": "Universal categories include the encoding of the grammatical relations of participants and predicates by distinguishing between their relations to a predicate, the encoding of temporal and spatial relations on predicates, and a system of grammatical person governing reference to and distinction between speakers and addressees and those about whom they are speaking.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": {
        "source_type": "page",
        "chunk_id": "page_0020",
        "title": "Grammatical categories",
        "url": null
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
